WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.652 --> 00:00:05.860
SAMIR HAMMOUDI: Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:06.840
My name is Samir.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.460
I'm a gaming solution
architect at Google Cloud.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.150
Today I will be introducing you
to Open Match, an open source

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.980
matchmaking framework
based on Kubernetes.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.490
Then Ishikawa-san from Grenge
will share about the experience

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.740
using Open Match
in "Kick-Flight,"

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:29.450
a new 4v4 mobile game that
just released last month.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:33.140
So before diving in to Grenge's
experience of Open Match,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.110
let's first take a look
at what exactly it is.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.510
But to explain to you
how it was founded,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.930
I first need to explain the
challenges that many developers

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.460
face with matchmaking services.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.750
Matchmaking is one of the
hardest unsolved problems

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.270
for online multiplayer games.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.570
At a fundamental level,
matchmaking takes players,

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.380
connect to your games, to
match them up and connect them

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.270
to game servers.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.910
Creating a good match is
critical to your game's

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.150
success.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.020
And what constitutes a good
match changes from game

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.730
to game.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:14.230
You have to consider attributes
like skills, level, latency,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.000
wait time.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.090
And they are all
critical to your game.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.060
In general, making a good
matchmaking service for a game

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.950
is hard, but it's core
to your game design.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.880
Running this as a global scale
for your player population

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.120
presents a whole different
set of challenges.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.130
Architecting a
matchmaking service

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.300
that can run at high skill,
reliably, is very hard,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.940
and may not be an
interesting problem

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.510
to solve for a game developer.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.230
This is exactly why
Open Match was created.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:51.680
So Open Match is an open
source matchmaking framework

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.630
co-founded with Unity.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.620
It leverages Kubernetes
to provide flexibility,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.770
scalability, and extensibility.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.070
Just to give you an example
of scale it can handle,

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:08.120
maybe you remember the
Halloween Doodle from 2018,

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.150
that 4v4 multiplayer game you
could play from the Google.com

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.590
website.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:16.080
Yeah, that one was using
Open Match to match players.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.050
And it handled a concurrent
peak of 500,000 players.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.820
Because, with Open
Match, you don't

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.640
need to worry about the
scalability and reliability

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.070
of the underlying
infrastructure,

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:34.950
you can focus on building the
core logic of your matchmaker.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.630
Because I think a picture
is worth a thousand words,

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.410
let's take a look at the
high-level architecture

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.730
of how online games will
integrate with Open Match.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.250
On the left side, you
have a game front end

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.930
with your core game logic.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.840
That calls into the
matchmaking framework.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.880
And at the same time,
on the right side,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.070
you have a pool of
servers that players

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.220
can be placed on that's
simultaneously calling

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.350
in the matchmaking logic.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.470
Open Match sits in the middle,
handling the players that

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:09.120
are available, splitting
them by skill level, latency,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.820
et cetera, and match
them up with servers

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.290
that are available.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.930
As a developer, you only care
about developing the match

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:21.100
function, which contains the
core logic of your matchmaker.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.790
The left and right boxes
are component from your game

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.150
that you need to
integrate with Open Match.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.420
Let's take a closer
look at those components

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.090
in the left and right boxes.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.310
The game front end is the
endpoint where your game

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.040
clans will connect to.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.170
To avoid cheating, you
don't want them to directly

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.450
connect to the Open
Match front end,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.080
because only your game front
end is capable of verifying

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.050
the data provided
by the game clans

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:53.660
by accessing a database
containing player's data.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.870
So basically it's your
game front end that

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.420
creates matchmaking requests--

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:02.750
also called tickets--
in Open Match.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.240
On the right side, you find
the game back end-- also

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.970
called director-- and the
dedicated game servers.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:12.440
Your game back end is a key
component in the matchmaker

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.990
since it triggers the
match function execution.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.450
Once Open Match
finds matches, it

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.360
will also handle the
assignment of game service

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.670
to the match players.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.470
This architecture is
something you can then

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:30.540
reuse across your games as
you build multiple games.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.050
To have a better idea of
how is the flow of a match,

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:39.100
let's take a look at the
life of a match step by step.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:43.050
First step, a player wants to
play against other players.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.470
The game clan makes a request
to your game front end

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.060
to find a match.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.890
It's the game front
end that actually

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.530
makes the request to
the Open Match front end

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.060
to find a match.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.990
Every request are stored in the
state store running on Redis.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.430
As I mentioned in
the previous slide,

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.330
it's the game back end that
triggers the match function

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.610
using the Open Match back end.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:09.370
The game back end
requests for a match.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.150
The Open Match back triggers
the matchmaking function

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.390
that uses the query component
to create Redis and find

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.860
candidates for a match.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.370
Once a match is found,
the Open Match back end

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.550
will return it to the game
back in that will then

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.640
finalize the entire matchmaking
process by assigning

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.850
a dedicated game server
to the match players.

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:35.120
The last step of the life of
a match is the assignment.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.580
It's the last step
where we assign a game

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.840
server to the matched players.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.740
For this, the game back end,
we allocate a dedicated game

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.810
server by using solutions like
Agones or any other custom

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.450
server management system.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:55.770
Once it gets the allocated
game server IP and port,

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.890
it will reach out again
to the Open Match back end

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.560
to update the tickets
stored in the state store

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.090
with that information.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.870
Finally, once tickets
have been updated,

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.830
the game front end will push
that game server details

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:13.290
to players, who will then
connect to the server and just

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.940
start to play.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.090
And, voila, that's how Open
Match works in a nutshell.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.930
Hope this was clear enough.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.760
If you're building
a small game, you

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.580
might not be interested in
a complex, highly-scalable

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.380
matchmaking framework.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:29.360
But you can still
leverage Open Match

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.000
to write a simple
matchmaking function that

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.200
will look like a single function
in the language of your choice.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.030
Plug that into the
framework, and you'll

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.400
have a fully functional
matchmaking service

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.110
without a lot of work.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:44.570
But where Open
Match really shines

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.720
is if you have a really
large system with thousands

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.920
or millions of players that are
all coming at the same time.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.730
And the player population is
segmented into different groups

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.950
and matched up very quickly.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.100
So you might be running
hundreds of concurrent matches

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.040
on the player population,
looking for the best match

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.290
to make your game work.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.080
So now that you know
what Open Match is,

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.930
let's listen to the experience
of the Japanese game studio

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:15.470
Grenge using Open Match in
their latest mobile game,

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:16.760
"Kick-Flight."

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:19.280
YASUNORI ISHIKAWA: Hi,
my name is Yasunori.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.620
I'm an engineering
manager at Grenge.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.670
Here to talk about why
and how we use Open Match

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.900
in "Kick-Flight."

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.390
Our experience is a game
called "Kick-Flight."

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:36.200
"Kick-Flight" is a free-flying
and midair battle action game

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.580
for smartphones.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.410
Four-versus-four real-time
multiplayer game.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:46.130
You control a character
called a kicker.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:50.150
12 characters with
three game modes.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:55.610
Available in more
than 130 countries.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:58.670
Launched on February 13, 2020.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:02.037
[ROCK MUSIC]

00:08:02.037 --> 00:08:07.328
[GAME SOUND EFFECTS]

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.890
Here are "Kick-Flight"'s
matchmaking requirements.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:53.450
The video on the left shows the
matchmaking using Open Match.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.830
Using the kicker
under Game Mode,

00:08:55.830 --> 00:09:00.840
and by tapping the Battle
button, matchmakings begin.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:04.330
"Kick-Flight" will match
two teams of four with

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:06.550
the following requirements--

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:10.420
match users with
similar play skills.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:14.230
Matching users with far
different play skills

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:18.080
will result in a
one-sided match.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.940
Minimize character
overlaps in battles.

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:24.130
Each of the
characters has a role.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.590
There are four types--

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:31.200
attack, support,
tank, and speed.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.930
Matchmaking is done so that the
same character or role does not

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:40.410
suffer as much as possible.

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:45.300
You can cancel any time
before matchmaking is found.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.630
The progress is displayed in
real time during matchmaking.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:54.900
There is four reasons why we
decided to use Open Match.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:58.030
The first one is
because we wanted

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:01.690
to make a general-purpose
matchmaking framework,

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:06.840
because building a matchmaker
for each game is pricey.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.360
Building a general-purpose
matchmaking

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.240
with a budget of a
game is not possible.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:17.170
As a result, each game has
a different matchmaking

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:22.170
implementation, depending
on the type of matchmaking.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.480
Making a matchmaker
game with one game app

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:31.200
can take anywhere from
six months to a year.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:37.110
Even in that case, there are
many problems about scaling.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:40.030
In "Kick-Flight", the
development of matchmaking

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:44.160
logic was about
one or two months.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:50.880
And it took about one month for
version updates and load tests.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.640
Most importantly,
this knowledge can

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:59.400
be used to build faster using
Open Match in the future.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:05.640
Secondly, because we wanted to
focus on the matchmaking logic,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:11.710
in general, scalability is a
major issue in matchmaking.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:14.680
This problem was minimized
because Open Match

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:18.520
is, by design, scalable.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:25.050
Third, because we could
find working MM examples.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:29.310
At that time, there
wasn't much documentation,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:32.916
but there were some
working examples.

00:11:32.916 --> 00:11:39.710
We did the source code, and
got the help of Googlers.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:44.690
The last one is because Open
Match was used successfully

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:48.930
in the Halloween Doodle in 2018.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.900
This is "Kick-Flight"
architecture.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:58.370
Except for Realtime Game Server,
everything is built on GCP.

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:01.400
The central part is Open Match.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.590
Creation and the
addition of tickets

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:06.860
are performed from
the API Server

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:09.440
through Cloud Load Balancer.

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.630
A creation of
assignment is requested

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:16.726
to Open Match via
Ingress and Endpoints.

00:12:16.726 --> 00:12:22.720
So therefore, Open Match
uses various parts on GKE.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:26.820
We have changed that
to Cloud Memorystore.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:32.830
As a side note, we used Cloud
Spanner as our main database.

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:37.100
In "Kick-Flight," there
are three main components.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:40.400
The API changes the kicker
and enhances the skill

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:43.910
of a character, called a disc.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:51.420
It is implemented using PHP7,
and it uses Cloud Memorystore

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:58.210
for Redis for session cache and
Cloud Spanner for player data.

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:01.240
Open Match's version
is version 0.7,

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:04.080
with some customization added.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:08.680
It handles players matchmaking,
and matchmaking function

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:10.720
is also implemented in PHP7.

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.980
Dedicated game server
handles the real-time aspect

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:18.960
of battles.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:24.060
Let me show you what went
well with Open Match.

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.480
We are able to focus on the
development of the matchmaking

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:28.800
logic.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.350
Open Match does the
communication part

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:34.140
with the client and the scale.

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:40.260
So we are able to concentrate on
the matchmaking logic, scaling

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:44.740
the system based on
the number of players.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:48.490
We can scale by increasing
front end and the matchmaking

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:51.180
function.

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:56.490
Also easily modify the
matchmaking criteria.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:14:01.580
Since front end and match
function are separated,

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:03.380
just modify the logic.

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:07.790
Then build and
deploy the images.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:12.480
We can deploy
without any downtime.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:14.680
The server management
was simplified,

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:19.150
thanks to the managed services
like GKE and the Cloud

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:21.530
Memorystore.

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:24.740
We used Cloud Endpoints
to secure the access

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:28.470
to the Open Match front end.

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:32.990
It was very easy to use
with GKE, because it

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:36.140
was made for Kubernetes.

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:41.250
By default, Redis
in GKE is used.

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:46.830
But management is easier
using crowd Memorystore.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:51.380
This helped reduce
operation cost.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:55.560
Assignment communication is
done via Cloud Endpoints.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:01.330
Originally, it should be done
via our game server front end.

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:07.290
But handling streaming
connection in PHP is complex.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:13.470
So we used Ingress and Cloud
Endpoints to handle this part.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:16.620
Finally, for
monitoring, Open Match

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:21.750
telemetry worked seamlessly
with Stackdriver.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:27.420
Here are a few changes
we faced with our game.

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:31.420
We launched our game using
Open Match for the first time

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.620
in the world.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:37.880
We used very early
versions, from version 0.3,

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:39.860
of Open Match.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:46.150
Back in version 0.3, there
was not much documentation.

00:15:46.150 --> 00:15:51.430
So we read source codes
with help from Googlers.

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:55.760
Here is some tuning we have
done on Open Match from

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:58.240
"Kick-Flight."

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:01.732
Parallel processing
by splitting profile.

00:16:01.732 --> 00:16:06.140
A match profile is Open
Match's representation

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:09.080
of a match specification.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:12.460
For example,
processing is performed

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:15.070
in parallel for each game mode.

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:18.390
We adjusted the retry intervals.

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:21.590
If the retry interval
is too short,

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:25.380
the load on the Redis
server will increase.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:29.030
Increasing this interval
can reduce the load

00:16:29.030 --> 00:16:31.880
on the Redis server.

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:36.530
We also adjusted the Redis TTLs.

00:16:36.530 --> 00:16:40.740
Longer TTL will
increase data volume.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:46.710
The shorter the TTL, the lower
the load on the Redis server.

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:50.270
We use C2 instances.

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:55.550
It is important to match
in a short period of time,

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:59.360
as it will not be able to
judge if the matchmaking

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:02.560
queue accumulates.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:05.440
For that purpose,
very fast processing

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:09.930
is realized using C2 instances.

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:14.650
For example, the difference
between 1.0 seconds

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:19.960
and 0.8 seconds will
make a big difference.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:24.890
We reduced the filter
condition of profile.

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:29.040
The load on increased
according to the multiplication

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:33.160
of the learning
profile and filter.

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:36.300
So we changed to minimum
filter conditions.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:41.640
We back-ported from version 0.8
because execution from back end

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:45.000
to function was not balanced.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:50.320
In addition, we performed load
test of communication path

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:52.070
using Locust.

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:56.380
Finally, let's look
back at the game launch.

00:17:56.380 --> 00:18:00.310
We are able to handle the
load at the time of launch

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:02.690
flawlessly.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:05.270
During our load
test, we measured

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:12.720
Open Match could handle at
least 250 entries per second.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:18.360
We had no incident caused
by Open Match so far.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:24.260
We developed our own solution
to increasing Redis data.

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:31.110
Open Match version 0.10
will include this fix.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:36.090
In conclusion, we are very
satisfied with Open Match.

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.820
We could accelerate the
game development speed,

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:43.080
and we could provide a very
fast yet scalable matchmaking

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:46.390
service to our players.

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:50.500
We plan to use Open Match
much for our next games

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:56.350
and even expand it to other
game studios in our group.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:19:00.540
Lastly, if you are
interested in Open Match,

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:05.920
I recommend looking at the
website, openmatch.dev,

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:10.150
where you can find great
documentation and many examples

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:13.440
to get started with.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:17.025
I can't wait to see what you
can come up with with Open

00:19:17.025 --> 00:19:18.840
Match in your game.

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:22.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

